# Default-route priority for every uplink on the board: lowest metric wins.
#
# This is the single source of truth. Before it existed the numbers were spread
# over four owners - a hardcoded "metric 50" in dhclient-script, a bare
# "route add default gw" with no metric at all in its TIMEOUT branch,
# wlan1-fix-metric.sh (which slept 3s and hoped dhclient had finished), and
# dhclient-exit-hooks - and no file anywhere stated the intended order, so the
# only way to learn it was to read all four and guess.
#
# Read by /usr/sbin/iface-metric, which is what dhclient-script and usbstart.sh
# call. Interfaces not listed here get the documented fallback in that script;
# nothing needs to be listed to work, listing it only sets its priority.
#
# The kernel picks the lowest-metric default route for forwarded packets, and
# netpolicy's NAT rules match at packet time, so changing a number here is the
# whole of changing which uplink the board and its LAN clients use. Leave gaps:
# a new uplink should be insertable without renumbering.
#
# Not listed on purpose: tun*/tap*. A VPN takes the default route only if its own
# config lets the server push one, and "redirect-gateway def1" installs
# 0.0.0.0/1 + 128.0.0.0/1, which beat any default route regardless of metric. So
# the tunnel is not part of this ladder - see workplans/002-routing-model.md.
#
# iface   metric
eth0      100
eth0:1    100
wlan1     200
wwan0     700
usb0      2000
